orking here  alone  all this spawns a multi-million dollar  smuggling racket and black market in  madagascar  little of which makes its way into these  miners pockets but which massively  contributes to deforestation affects  wildlife and weather patterns as well as  the climate  this is just one illegal encampment in  one part of the country there are an  estimated half a million people who are  taking part in illegal mining if you  look here you can see it goes on for  some distance that would make it the  second biggest employer in the whole of  madagascar and more and more people are  being driven into it because of poverty  and of course that has a big impact on  the habitat  the miners are strikingly positive  despite their lack of success and the  frayed equipment they rely on doesn't  seem to influence their nerves  ali and his crew are unperturbed by the  dangers or the impact on their  homeland's precious environment any laws  preventing this illegal mining are  unenforced  they've all spent all their lives doing  this and say they've no alternative  i'm not afraid at all he says i'm used  to this work i make my living out of it  and i'm not afraid  [Applause]  but the forests are coming down at an  unrelenting pace in this one of the most  biodiverse nations on earth  this logger told us he expected to bring  down a hundred trees that day and he's  in a team of ten  the trees are used for firewood for  cooking and selling as well as making  way for illegal mining but every loss of  every tree adds carbon dioxide to the  air affecting the climate and  endangering wildlife and again poverty  is the driver  robert will make ten dollars today  five times more than most of his fellow  citizens the bulk of whom are living  below the poverty line  back at the illegal mining site a gem  buyer is driving a hard bargain  no good  there's a crack in it he says  this group of families have been working  five months to collect this amount of  barrel the gem buyer offers five  thousand nariari  there's nothing good here he says but  famer in the lime green shirt is  disgusted he's got 15 relatives to feed  he's being offered just over a dollar  for months of work  he's not selling today  the gem buyer shows us what he considers  good quality precious barrel gems  he reckons he'll get 25 for all this a  fraction of what they'll fetch when  they're turned into rings or earrings  and at an enormous cost to madagascar's  unique environment  alex crawford sky news in madagascar
 

